{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: requirements.authoring.usage_inputs is satisfied.** The implementation covers every obligation and the tests exercise the interesting cases. I have two minor reservations about marking and aggregation, set out at the end.\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Mandatory inputs, sizes and kinds.**\n  - `UsageLog::prepared` (`src/authoring/usage.rs`) pushes one `InputUsage` per input, with `origin`, `kind`, `bytes`, `lines`, `components`, `unique`, `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request`.\n  - `kind` is one of `outline`, `requirements`, `builtin` or `definition`.\n  - `bytes` is the sum of `str::len` over the text lines plus the newline separators, so it counts UTF-8 bytes. `lines` is the length of the text array.\n  - `components` is `matches.len()`, which gives the number of matched declarations for a requirements input.\n  - The first request's mandatory inputs are recorded by `assess_subject` with `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", …)`.\n  - Follow-up conversations (second assessment, verification) record theirs in `followup_sources`.\n- **Additional inputs.** `converse` calls `usage.prepared(<tool name>, &delivery.inputs)` for every successful tool delivery. Inputs from `habeas` and `ripgrep`, including the terminology they add, get the same size, kind and line records, with the tool name as `origin`.\n- **Unique versus repeated reads.** `unique` comes from `self.seen.insert(input.to_string())`, so any repeat of an identical input is flagged `unique: false`. That includes a repeat of a mandatory input.\n- **Successful versus failed reads.** `UsageLog::tool(name, failed)` increments `tool_calls` and, on failure, `tool_failures`. Unknown tool names are folded into \"unknown\" by `tool_name`. `converse` marks `dispatch` errors as failed. Failed reads add no input record.\n- **Prepared versus sent.** `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on all prepared inputs. `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the provider returned usage, so a failed request leaves inputs unsent. Retries then set the later index.\n- **File discovery.** `list_files` returns `inputs: Vec::new()` (`tools.rs`), so nothing is recorded as an input. `converse` still calls `usage.tool(\"list_files\", …)`, so it counts as a tool call.\n\n**Tests**\n- **`input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source`** covers:\n  - kind;\n  - UTF-8 byte count (using \"λ\");\n  - line count;\n  - `components == 2` for a requirements input;\n  - repeated reads (`unique` true, then false);\n  - the `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request` transitions;\n  - no source content in the stored usage.\n- **`failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt`** checks that a failed request leaves `sent_in_request` null until the retry succeeds.\n- **`throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt`** checks that the retry request is the sent one (submitted in request 0, sent in request 8).\n- **`tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text`** covers tool call, failure and skip counts.\n- **The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free`** goes end to end:\n  - `list_files` is counted as a tool call with no `list_files` input;\n  - `ripgrep` and `habeas` inputs have bytes and lines greater than zero;\n  - there is a unique `habeas` input and a repeated one (`unique == false`, `sent_in_request == 1`);\n  - a failed `habeas` read shows up in `tool_failures`;\n  - a mandatory definition input has bytes greater than zero;\n  - no source text leaks into the usage record.\n- **Tool-level tests** assert that `list_files`, `outline` and `references` deliver no inputs.\n\n**Reservations (not enough to fail)**\n- Counts \"by evidence kind\" are stored as per-input records with a `kind` field, not as pre-aggregated totals. They are trivially derivable, and I treat that as satisfying the requirement.\n- The `seen` field and the `inputs` field in `UsageLog` and `UsageRecord` carry only `usage_storage` or `usage_requests` markers, not `usage_inputs`. The behavior is implemented in the marked `prepared`, `begin_request` and `end_request`, so I treat this as a minor marking omission.\n- The `builtin` kind is an extra category beyond evidence kinds. It is harmless and is justified by the mandatory-inputs handling of built-ins.",
  "references": "dc43fb7d9d6f3d57673af4f00f38c1477c5deda399f79d1bccb7a3770e5c7190",
